WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.550
Let's get started.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.590
Today, we're going to
talk about evaluation.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.150
This is one of these topics
that I think looks simple

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.300
but actually is far from it.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.390
Mechanically, it's just
given a fixed model.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.620
Ask the question,
how good is it?

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.840
So seems pretty easy enough.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.760
And if you think
about evaluation,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.600
you probably see
a lot of things,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.590
such as benchmark scores.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.870
So for example, papers that
put out language models,

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.920
put out some benchmarks,
scores on various benchmarks

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.430
like MMLU, Amy Codeforces.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.300
Here's a Llama 4 paper.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.980
They evaluate on MMLU Pro,
MATH 500, GPQA, at least

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.250
for language.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.960
And then there's some
multimodal stuff.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.410
If you look at OLMo,
it's kind of MATH MMLU

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.610
and then some other things
like DROP and GSM8k, and so on.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.720
And see all these numbers.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.640
Most language
models are evaluated

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.310
on roughly the same
benchmarks, but not quite.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.150
But what are these
benchmarks, and what

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.110
do these numbers actually mean?

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.450
So here is another
example from HELM,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.490
where we have a bunch of
different standard benchmarks

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.230
which are all collated
together, which

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.710
is something we'll talk
about a little bit later.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:41.490
There's also benchmarks that
look at the costs, not just

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.370
the accuracy score.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.700
So Artificial Analysis is this
website that does, I think,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.960
a fairly good job of looking
at these Pareto frontiers

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.870
where they have this
intelligence index, which

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:56.880
is basically a combination of
different benchmarks and then

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.160
the price that you would have to
pay per token to use that model.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.970
And of course, O3
is really good,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.045
but it's also really expensive.

00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:11.170
And apparently, I guess
some of these other models,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.587
actually, according
to this index,

00:02:12.587 --> 00:02:18.120
are at least as good and
much cheaper, it seems.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.130
And maybe another
way to look at it

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.230
is a model is good if
people choose to use it.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:26.100
So Open Router is
this website that

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.093
essentially has
traffic that gets

00:02:28.093 --> 00:02:29.260
routed to a bunch of models.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.250
So they have data on which
models people are choosing.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.890
And so if you just look
at the number of tokens

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.710
that are sent to each model,
you can define a leaderboard,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.610
and you can take a leap
of faith and assume

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.760
that people are choosing
the models that are good.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.390
So according to this, then
OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.120
seem to be at the top.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.940
Here's another
one, Chatbot Arena,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.155
which I think is very popular.

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:02.780
I'll talk a little
bit more about this,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.640
but yeah, it's another
ranking between models

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.550
where people on the
internet have conversations

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:13.930
with these models and express
their pairwise preferences.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.690
So there's a lot of
numbers and rankings

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.270
that I'm just kind
of throwing at you.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:26.270
And then you see these vibes
where people post on X. Hey,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.860
look at this awesome example
of something the language model

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.240
can do.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.050
There's a lot of these
examples out there.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:41.390
So that's another source of
data on how good models are.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.330
But really I think
Andrej Karpathy

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.660
did a good job of assessing
the current situation, which

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.140
is that there is an
evaluation crisis.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:55.330
There are some benchmarks
like MMLU, which apparently

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.830
were good to look at.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.900
But now, the
underlying assumption

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.740
is that maybe they have been
either saturated or gamed

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.680
or something in between.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.130
And then there's problems with
the Chatbot Arena, which we'll

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.120
talk about a little bit later.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.570
And so really, we
have all these models.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:21.279
We have this plethora of
benchmarks and numbers

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:25.550
that are coming out,
and it's unclear,

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:30.010
I think, at this point,
which are the right way

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.680
to do evaluation.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.540
You notice a pattern
in this class where

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:41.890
everything is kind of messy,
and evaluation is no different.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:48.190
So in this class, I want to
talk a little bit about how you

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.920
wish to think about evaluation.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.730
And then I'm going to go through
a bunch of different benchmarks

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:58.496
and talk about a few
issues with benchmarks.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:08.290
So evaluation, at some level,
is just a mechanical process.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.790
You take an existing model.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.100
You don't really worry
about how it was trained.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.780
And then you throw
prompts at it.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.450
You get some responses.

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:19.360
You compute some metrics,
and you average the numbers.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.330
So it seems like a kind of a
quick script that you can write.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:28.450
But actually, evaluation is
really kind of a profound topic.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:31.860
And it also determines
how language models are

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.670
going to be built, because
people build these evaluations,

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.910
and the top language
model developers

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.248
are tracking these over time.

00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:41.790
And if you track
something and you're

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.357
trying to get your
number to go up,

00:05:43.357 --> 00:05:44.940
it's going to really
influence the way

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.020
that you develop your model.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.750
So that's why
evaluation, I think,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:53.540
is really maybe a leading
indicator of where

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.340
things are going to go.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:58.230
So what's the point
of evaluation?

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.160
Why do we even do it?

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.370
So the answer is that there
is no one true evaluation.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.383
It depends on what question
you're trying to answer.

00:06:09.383 --> 00:06:11.300
And this is an important
point because there's

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.542
no such thing as, oh, I'm
just evaluating a model.

00:06:13.542 --> 00:06:14.250
You get a number.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.710
But what does that
number tell you?

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.110
And does it actually answer
your original question?

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:22.500
So here are some examples of
what you might want to do.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.683
So suppose you're a
user or a company,

00:06:25.683 --> 00:06:27.600
and you're trying to
make a purchase decision.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.110
So you can use either use
cloud or you can use Grok

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.090
or you can use Gemini or O3.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:41.510
And which one should you choose
for your particular use case?

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:45.437
Another is that your
researcher, you're

00:06:45.437 --> 00:06:47.520
not actually trying to use
the model for anything.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.730
You just want to know what
are the raw capabilities

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:51.340
of the model?

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.410
Are we making scientific
progress on an AI?

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.740
So that's a much
more general question

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.760
that's not anchored to
any particular use case.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.220
And then policymakers
and businesses

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.980
might want to just
understand objectively,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.730
at a given point in time, what
are the benefits and harms

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.120
of a model?

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.100
Where are we?

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.680
Are models giving us--
telling us the right answer?

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.410
How are they helping?

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.760
How much value are
they delivering?

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.010
Model developers might
be doing evaluation

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.460
because they want to get
feedback to improve the model.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.340
They might evaluate and see,
oh, this score is too low.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:34.620
So let's try an intervention.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:35.680
And it goes up.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.970
Therefore, we keep
the intervention.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:41.820
Evaluation is often used
in the development cycle

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.430
of language models as well.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.400
So in each case,
there is some goal

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.830
that the evaluator
wants to achieve,

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:56.050
and this needs to be translated
into a concrete evaluation.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.220
And the concrete
evaluation you choose

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.750
will depend on what
you're trying to achieve.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.660
So in evaluation, there's--
here's a simple framework you

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.580
can think about.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.140
So what are the
inputs, the prompts?

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:20.460
How do you call
the language model?

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.160
And then once a language
model produces outputs,

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.750
how do you assess the outputs?

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.260
And then how do you
interpret the results?

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.150
So let's look at each
of these questions.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:35.340
So the inputs-- so where do
you get the set of prompts?

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.659
Which use cases are covered
by-- by your prompts?

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.309
That's a question.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:45.780
Do they have representation
of the tails?

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.540
Do they have difficult inputs
that challenge the model,

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.380
or are they vanilla easy
cases that any language

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.180
model would be able to do?

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.720
And then finally, in the
multi-turn chatbot setting,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.540
the inputs are actually
dependent on the model.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.580
So that introduces
a complication.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.620
And even in the
single-term setting,

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:09.790
you might be wanting to
choose inputs that are

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:11.780
tailored to the model as well.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.480
So the question of inputs.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.920
And then how do you
call a language model?

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.310
So there's many ways to
prompt a language model.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:23.620
You can do few shot, zero
shot, chain of thought.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:27.250
And we'll see that
each of these decisions

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:30.430
actually introduces
a lot of variance

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:33.110
into how the valuation metric.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:37.160
So language models are still
very sensitive to the prompt,

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:43.480
which means that evaluation
needs to take that into account.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:47.570
And the particular type
of strategy you're using

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.130
is something that
you have to decide,

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.780
whether you have tool
use for arithmetic,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:58.340
or you're able to do RAG if--
or use a tool if you are doing

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.340
some of recent knowledge query.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:04.640
And then finally, I think
we'll talk about agents

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:07.140
in a-- a little bit later.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.320
Are we even evaluating?

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.980
What is the object
of evaluation?

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.700
Are we evaluating
a language model,

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.850
or are we evaluating
the whole system?

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:19.910
And this is also an
important distinction

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.780
because the model
developer might

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:26.360
want to evaluate the
former because they're

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.320
trying to make their
language model better,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.080
and the agentic system
and the scaffolding

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.110
is just a means to
derive the metric.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:37.070
But the user doesn't
care what you're doing

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.545
with what language model using.

00:10:38.545 --> 00:10:40.170
There might be multiple
language model.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:43.760
They just care about
the system as a whole.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:49.610
And then finally, the outputs.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.150
How do you evaluate outputs?

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.140
Often, you have
reference outputs.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.510
And are these clean?

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:58.800
Are they error-free?

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.740
Very basic question,
but we'll see later

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.600
that that's not
obviously the case.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:05.990
What metrics do you use
for code generation?

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:07.580
Is it pass at one?

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.665
Is it pass at 10?

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.990
How do you factor into the cost?

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:17.130
Because you see a lot
of the leaderboards,

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.910
they're completely--

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:22.350
the cost is kind of
marginalized away.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.840
So you don't have a sense of
maybe the top model is actually

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.730
10 times more expensive than
the second model, for example.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:34.640
And that's why Pareto frontiers
are generally good to look at.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.940
And obviously, in
some use cases,

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.100
not all errors
are created equal.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.580
And how do you incorporate that
into your evaluation criteria?

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.660
And opening a
generation is obviously

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.220
tricky to evaluate because
there's no ground truth.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.140
[? During ?] some text,
write me a compelling story

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.340
about Stanford.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:01.050
That's how you evaluate that.

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:04.150
So suppose you get
through all those.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.500
Now you have the metrics.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.160
And how do you interpret it?

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:11.770
So suppose you get a 91 number.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:15.240
Does that mean it's good?

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.940
If you're a company, do you
deploy it to your users?

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.870
Is that good enough?

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.620
How do you determine if you're--
let's say, you're a researcher--

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:29.700
has this language model really
learned particular types

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.340
of generalization?

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:38.310
This requires us to confront
the issue of train test overlap.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:42.300
And then finally, we'll talk
a little bit about how--

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:45.010
again, what is the
object of the evaluation?

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.670
Is it the model or the system,
or is it actually the method?

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:52.470
So often, in research, the
output of the research paper

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.128
is a new method for
doing something.

00:12:54.128 --> 00:12:55.420
It's not necessarily the model.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:59.022
The model is just an example
application of a method.

00:12:59.022 --> 00:13:00.480
So if you're
evaluating the method,

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.610
then I think many of
the actual evaluations

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.770
that people do don't
really make sense

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:11.460
unless you have clear
controls on what you're doing.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.130
So in summary, there's
a lot of questions

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.358
to actually think through when
you're doing an evaluation.

00:13:17.358 --> 00:13:18.900
It's not just take
a bunch of prompts

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.930
and feed it into
a language model.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.390
Yeah, question.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:25.630
A question for inputs.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:29.520
It said that are the inputs
adapted to the model.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.520
So should they be adapted,
or shouldn't they be adapted?

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.620
So the question is, should the
inputs be adapted to the model?

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:41.450
Again, this depends on
what you're trying to do.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.300
So in some cases,
like the multi-turn,

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.520
they have to be
adapted to the model.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:51.490
I think it's not realistic
to have a static chatbot

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:55.310
evaluation where you have user
assistant, user assistant,

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.870
but the assistant
is someone else.

00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:00.070
And you're meant to
respond because you

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.950
might be put in a
kind of a weird spot

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:04.510
that you would never
get into if you

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:07.480
were driving the conversation.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:12.550
In red teaming, it's helpful
to adapt the evaluation

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:16.300
to the model because you're
looking for these very rare tail

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.640
events, and you're
just going to be

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:22.275
very inefficient if you're just
generically generating prompts.

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:29.510
But of course, when you adapt
your evaluation to the model,

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:34.240
now how do you compare it
between different models?

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.120
So there's a trade-off there.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.220
Have any other
questions on this kind

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:44.230
of broad kind of
conceptual level before we

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:45.075
dive into details?

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:49.210
Yeah.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:51.710
I think it's something
that we've relied on so far

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:55.870
is that perplexity seems to
be informative about a lot

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:59.510
of capabilities that models
improve, all these capabilities.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:03.650
And I'm curious if in the
natural language setting,

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:06.940
are there any sets of
these questions that

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:10.990
don't have that strong
relationship that

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:14.900
don't seem to be improving
well as we improve perplexity?

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.680
Or does that somewhat
genuinely [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.660
enough to convince yourself
that you're improving?

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:24.880
Yeah, so the question is,
is perplexity all you need,

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.010
or are there some things that
aren't captured by perplexity?

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:31.940
So that's actually a good
segue to talk about perplexity.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.700
But to answer your
question more directly,

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.080
so Tatsu showed a
slide, I think last--

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.500
maybe last lecture
that was looking

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.110
at the correlation between
perplexity and downstream task

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:42.990
performance.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:47.280
And it was all over the place,
at least in that setting.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.890
So it's not always the case
that perplexity is correlated

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.580
with the thing you care about.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:59.600
That said, I think what has been
shown is that over long enough

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:04.970
time, like over multiple
scales, perplexity does globally

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:09.020
correspond to everything
improving because the stronger

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:11.390
models are strong
at most things,

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:19.040
and the small 1B models are just
worse on most things overall.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:24.320
And yeah, so maybe I'll say
a bit more about perplexity.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:28.010
So remember that a language
model is a distribution

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.520
over sequences of tokens.

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:33.440
Perplexity measures essentially
whether the language model

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:36.210
is assigning high
probability to some data set,

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.170
so you can define the perplexity
against a particular data

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:42.620
set, usually some sort
of validation set.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.530
So in pre-training, we're
minimizing the perplexity

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:48.380
of the training set.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.320
So the natural thing is when
you're evaluating a language

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:55.040
model, you want to evaluate
the perplexity on the test set.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:17:00.530
The standard thing is
having an IED split.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:05.810
So and this is indeed how
language modeling research

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:09.270
was in the last decade.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:15.020
So in the 2010s, there were
various standard data sets

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:16.603
for language modeling.

00:17:16.603 --> 00:17:18.770
So there's a Penn Treebank,
which actually goes back

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:23.910
to the '90s Wikitext, a
1 billion word benchmark,

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:25.500
which came from
machine translation.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:32.100
And it has a lot of translated
government proceedings and news.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:36.250
And so these are the data
sets that people used.

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:38.850
And generally,
what you did was--

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.170
I have-- I'm an LLM researcher.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:43.230
I'd try and pick one of these.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.730
I pick Wall Street Journal.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:49.440
I train on the designated
training split,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.150
and I evaluate on
Wall Street Journal

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:52.420
the designated test split.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:58.080
And I look at the accuracy.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:02.430
There's a bunch of
work in the 2010s.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.100
This was the transition
between n-gram models.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:08.910
And then there was people
mixing in neural with n-gram,

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.390
and there's all sorts of things.

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:15.210
And I think that one of the--
the most prominent results

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:18.840
in the mid-2010s was this
paper from Google that

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:22.090
showed if you design
the architecture right,

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:24.350
you can actually--
and scale up, you

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:26.920
can actually dramatically
reduce the perplexity.

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.920
So if you think about
51 to 30, that's

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:32.920
like a massive
perplexity reduction.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:37.310
And so to go back to what
questions you're asking,

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:39.490
the perplexity of
this game was really

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:43.750
helpful for advancing
language modeling research

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:45.380
because it was a
challenge problem.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.838
One of the points
in this paper was

00:18:46.838 --> 00:18:50.290
that on the smaller data
sets, people were worried

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:51.770
about overfitting and all that.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:55.220
In the larger data sets, you
just have a different game.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:19:00.370
The game was to even
just fit the data at all.

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:05.470
And then GPT-1, GPT-2,
I think, changed the way

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:10.360
that people viewed perplexity
or language model evaluations.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:14.298
So remember, GPT-2 trained
on 40 gigabytes of text.

00:19:14.298 --> 00:19:16.340
These were websites that
were linked from Reddit.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:20.590
And then you just
evaluate directly on--

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:25.640
no fine tuning, directly on the
standard perplexity benchmarks.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.278
So this is clearly out of
distribution evaluation.

00:19:28.278 --> 00:19:30.070
You're training a web
text, and then you're

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.540
going to evaluate on wiki text.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:36.950
But the point is that the
training is broad enough--

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.230
web text is broad enough--

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:42.040
that you hope that you
get strong generalization.

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:45.040
So they showed a
[INAUDIBLE] table

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:49.960
like this, where you have
different sizes of the models,

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:52.760
and you have
different benchmarks.

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:54.670
So here they have
the Penn Treebank,

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:56.510
and you have wiki text.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:58.960
And you have one billion words.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:00.460
And you're looking
at the perplexity

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.260
on all these benchmarks.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:05.260
And at least on the
small data sets,

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:08.680
such as Penn Treebank,
which is kind of tiny,

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.810
they were actually able to get
beyond the state of the art.

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:14.660
So they didn't train on
Penn Treebank at all.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:16.660
And they were able to--
because they trained

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:19.270
on so much other data,
they were able to beat

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:20.930
this state of the art on that.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:23.980
Now, for one billion
words, they were still on--

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:27.170
above by quite a
bit because once you

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:29.235
have a large enough
data set, then

00:20:29.235 --> 00:20:30.860
just train it directly
on that data set

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:33.780
is going to be better than
trying to rely on transfer,

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:38.060
at least at some
1 billion scale.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:40.280
Yeah.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.810
If they're trained on
websites from Reddit,

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:47.230
how do you know you're not
including like Penn Treebank?

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:48.980
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:52.410
Yeah, so the question is, if
you're training on wet data,

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:55.370
how do you know you're not
just training on Penn Treebank?

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:58.910
So this is a huge
issue, in general.

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:01.050
Train tests overlap with
train test contamination.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:04.460
We'll talk about it a bit later.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:07.320
Typically, people just
do the decontamination.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.730
So they take their test set,
and they remove any document

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:15.980
or paragraph or whatever that
has a 13-gram overlap with

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:17.390
the test set.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:20.570
Now there's subtleties
there because there

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:24.080
might be like slight
paraphrases that still

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:26.780
might be-- like near
duplicates don't get detected,

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:29.160
and it's messy.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:33.380
There's also even cases where
you might get math problems that

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:37.400
are translating into another
language which have no overlap

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:38.870
but still are--

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.710
essentially, if you
have the answer,

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:43.520
language models are good
enough that they can translate

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:45.840
in their heads false positives.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:49.610
It's one thing
supporting another?

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:52.560
If you have also tons
of false positives,

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:56.270
if you have training sets
that quote the test set.

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:57.170
Yeah.

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:57.870
Yeah.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:02.840
So that generally
is-- oh, I mean,

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:06.590
it's better to be
conservative here

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:08.070
because there's
so much web text.

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:11.880
If you didn't train on some
core text and you still do,

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.650
well, then I think that's fine.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:16.850
You just don't want to
overpromise your model

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:18.470
performance here.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:23.340
Yeah, so that's something
we'll come back to.

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:23.840
Yeah.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.190
[INAUDIBLE] train on
the smaller data set,

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:29.010
we can train on a
large data set is still

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:36.900
in large part [INAUDIBLE]
a small problem.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.410
So the question is, can
you distill a large model

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.330
into a smaller model?

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:46.120
Instead of the train the
large model on a small set--

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:46.620
Yeah.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:49.800
--we train the large
data set [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:53.190
still model performance.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:57.930
So here the model size
isn't really something

00:22:57.930 --> 00:22:59.320
that we're too worried about.

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:01.000
You get to choose
any model size.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:04.290
In fact, I think compute
budget isn't really

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.580
the standard size here.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:09.160
It's just more about
data efficiency.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.310
You're given this data set.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:17.345
Can you get the best perplexity
on these standard data sets?

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.820
Yeah, so this is the
shift of what it means

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:25.420
to evaluate language models.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:27.960
And then since GPT-2
and GPT-3 language

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:31.133
modeling papers have shifted
more towards downstream task

00:23:31.133 --> 00:23:32.550
accuracy-- so most
of this lecture

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:34.720
is going to be
about some of task,

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:39.030
but I want to put in a
plug for perplexity still.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:42.750
So perplexity, I
think, is still useful

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:46.110
because for several
reasons, it's

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:48.840
smoother than
downstream task accuracy

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:52.530
because you're getting all
these fine-grained logits

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:54.270
and probabilities
of individual tokens

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:56.970
rather than just I
generated some stuff,

00:23:56.970 --> 00:24:00.810
and is it correct or wrong?

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:05.730
And it turns out that all the
scaling stuff is done generally

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:10.440
with perplexity of some
sort because it allows

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.200
you to more gracefully fit these
curves rather than otherwise,

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.290
you get these kind
of discontinuities.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:19.410
And it won't be quite linear.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.490
The other thing is, which I'll
talk a little bit later about,

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.290
is perplexity.

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:28.420
In some senses, you're
universal in the sense

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:31.168
that you're paying
attention to every token

00:24:31.168 --> 00:24:32.210
that you have a data set.

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:34.100
You're only paying
attention to every token.

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:39.500
Whereas task accuracy, you
might miss some nuances.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:43.760
In particular, you can
get an answer correct,

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.420
but for the wrong
reasons, especially

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:47.883
if your data set is gameable.

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:56.440
Now note that perplexity is
still useful even in down task--

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:02.170
downstream tasks as well,
because you can essentially

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:05.530
condition on the prompt
and look at the probability

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:07.220
of the answer.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:13.130
So there's some Scala
papers that do this.

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:17.140
So instead of relying
just on validation loss

00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:19.120
on some sort of
corpus, they look

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:21.640
at downstream tasks, which
they care about and fit

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:25.150
scaling laws directly for that.

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:28.180
So one caveat about
perplexity-- and this

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:31.990
is from the
perspective-- suppose

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.870
you're running a leaderboard.

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:37.570
People are submitting
their models,

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:42.490
and you want to report
their perplexities.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:45.640
Now there's a sort
of a dilemma here

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:49.810
because you need to
trust the language model

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:51.230
provider to some extent.

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:54.080
So if you're just
doing task accuracy,

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:55.730
you just take the model.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:58.550
You run it, and then you
get the generate output.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:01.210
And then now you
have your code that

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:04.463
evaluates a generated output
against the reference.

00:26:04.463 --> 00:26:05.630
And it could be exact match.

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:06.380
It could be F1.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:08.440
It could be something else.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:10.040
And then you're fine.

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:13.010
So you don't really need to
look inside the black box.

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:15.430
But for perplexity, remember
the language model has

00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:17.750
to generate
probabilities, and you

00:26:17.750 --> 00:26:20.300
have to trust that they
are going to sum to 1,

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.970
So if you expose an
interface, which is give me

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.560
the probability
of this sequence,

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.530
then if they not
even maliciously.

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.670
They might just have a
bug where they assign

00:26:31.670 --> 00:26:35.100
probability 0.8 to everything.

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:39.350
And then they're going to look
really good, except for that's

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:41.870
not a valid distribution.

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:43.760
So that's just one
kind of caveat.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:48.470
And Perplexity evaluations
are very easy to screw up

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:49.950
if you're not careful.

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:51.487
Yeah, question.

00:26:51.487 --> 00:26:53.570
How can it be that you're
generating probabilities

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:55.700
like all of these [INAUDIBLE]?

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.190
So the question is,
how can you generate

00:26:58.190 --> 00:26:59.900
probabilities that are all 0.8?

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:03.410
That's if you have
a bug, for example.

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:04.920
I think it gets tricky.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:06.980
So for autoregressive
models, if your interface

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.990
is like you have to give me
the logits of all the words,

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:15.660
then I can verify myself
that they sum to 1.

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:18.040
But if I'm just
giving you, let's say,

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:19.980
the probability
of the next token

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:25.240
and you say 0.8, well, because
I'm giving you the token,

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:28.650
I don't have a way of verifying
that all the other tokens need

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:29.680
to sum to 1.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:30.990
Yeah.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:37.360
Just going to ask if
it's not [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:39.610
The question is, is it
standard to get all the logits?

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.700
So usually, if you're
computing perplexity,

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:44.830
you have fairly deep access,
and you're just like computing.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:47.910
And you look at the code,
and you make sure it's right.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:50.240
But you do have to double-check.

00:27:52.645 --> 00:27:53.145
Yeah.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:56.650
OK.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:28:00.040
So here on this
point about universe.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:04.020
So there are some
people in the world who

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:06.220
we call perplexity maximalists.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:08.620
And their view is as follows.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:11.370
So let's say your true
distribution is t,

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.470
and your model is p.

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:15.270
So the true distribution,
imagine it's

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:16.600
like this wonderful thing.

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:18.960
You have a prompt, and it
just magically gives you

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:21.940
the right answer and so on.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:25.590
And so in that case,
the best perplexity

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:28.800
you can get from a
model is kind of lower

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:32.950
bounded by entropy of t.

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:36.010
And that's exactly
when p equals t.

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.920
So this is basically
distribution matching.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:45.660
So by basically minimizing
the perplexity of P

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:47.820
with respect to t,
you're basically

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:50.500
forcing p to be as
close to t as possible.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:54.108
And in the limit, if you have
T, then you solve all the tasks.

00:28:54.108 --> 00:28:55.775
And you reach AGI,
and then you're done.

00:29:01.890 --> 00:29:04.950
The counter to this
is that this might not

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:08.040
be the most efficient
way to get there

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:09.780
because you might
be pushing down

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:12.610
on parts of the distribution
that just don't matter.

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:15.940
There's a reason we
define these tasks

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:19.640
in a certain way, because we
are curating what we care about,

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:22.450
rather than just
blindly matching

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.970
the probability of every single
token, which is something

00:29:24.970 --> 00:29:28.990
that I think clearly
humans don't have to do.

00:29:28.990 --> 00:29:33.370
But nonetheless,
perplexity-- or I

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:36.550
guess minimization has
been tremendously useful

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:38.390
for training.

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:43.240
And there's something I think
to this about evaluation

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:46.090
as well, especially
in light of how

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:47.890
benchmarks have been gameable.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.350
In some ways, like
perplexity, as long

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:53.025
as your train and
test are separate,

00:29:53.025 --> 00:29:56.565
it is not really a kind
of a gameable quantity.

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:02.680
OK, just to mention a few other
things that look like perplexity

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:04.040
but aren't perplexity.

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:06.850
So there's close
tasks where the idea

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.340
is that you get some
sentence, and you're

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:13.630
meant to complete the
filling in the missing word.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:17.410
So lambada is a task this,
where the context is chosen

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:19.330
to be particularly
challenging, and you

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:20.680
need to look at long contexts.

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:26.410
And you're supposed
to guess the word.

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:29.090
So this has been
kind of saturated.

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:31.900
So a lot of the tasks
that look like perplexity

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.960
have just been really
obliterated by a language model

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:41.048
because they're
basically perplexity.

00:30:41.048 --> 00:30:41.840
Here's another one.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.865
Hella swag where you--

00:30:43.865 --> 00:30:45.740
it's trying to get at
common sense reasoning.

00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:48.610
You have a sentence,
and you're trying

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:52.220
to pick the completion
that makes the most sense.

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:54.790
So this is essentially
the way you evaluate

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:58.390
is that you look at the
probability of each candidate

00:30:58.390 --> 00:31:03.220
given the prompt, and you're
just measuring the likelihood.

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.617
There's some wrinkle
with the normalizing

00:31:06.617 --> 00:31:07.700
over the number of tokens.

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:12.270
But more or less, this
is about perplexity.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:13.070
Yeah.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:16.280
So what is the goal
of the video here?

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:19.160
What do you use to train
the data [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:23.550
Yeah, so the question is, what
is the role of the video here?

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:24.050
Ignore that.

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:26.370
The data is completely all text.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.610
So the way that the
data was created

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:34.280
was to use ActivityNet and
then wiki how to mine the data.

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:35.700
Yeah, yeah.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:38.390
Actually, this is kind of--
brings me to this other point

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:38.960
about--

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:42.380
that's already been mentioned
about train task overlap, which

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:43.970
is WikiHow as a website.

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:47.780
And while there was
a bunch of processing

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:50.730
that happened to generate this
exact question from WikiHow,

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:52.790
if you go to WikiHow,
you'll see things

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:57.080
that look very much like the
hella swag training set, even--

00:31:57.080 --> 00:32:01.160
or [? the ?] data set,
even though it's not

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:03.620
like a kind of a verbatim match.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:06.010
So you have to be
very, very careful.

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:14.540
OK, so now let me go through
some standard knowledge, or just

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:20.150
benchmarks, that are popular
for evaluating language models.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:22.680
And for each one, I just
want to describe it--

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:25.740
I think talking about
where the data comes from,

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:29.720
where the state of
the art is, and so on.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:36.950
So MMLU, which is probably the
canonical standardized test

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:40.650
for language models by now,
that's actually quite old.

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:42.590
It's from 2020.

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:45.420
This was right after
GPT-3 came out.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:51.335
And at the time, this
was a little bit pretty--

00:32:51.335 --> 00:32:53.210
I think it was pretty
forward-looking because

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:56.150
at that time, the idea of
having a language model that

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:59.270
could zero shot, or even few
shot, a ton of different things,

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:00.860
was wild.

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:03.200
How would you get a
language model just

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:06.120
to solve all these
questions automatically?

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.390
But now it seems like, oh,
yeah. yeah, you just put it

00:33:09.390 --> 00:33:12.070
into ChatGPT, and it works.

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:13.690
But at that time,
it was not obvious.

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:18.270
So what they did was
they curated 57 subjects.

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:20.640
They're all
multiple-choice questions.

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:26.460
They were collected just from
the web, whatever that means.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.560
And so again, train
test overlap--

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:32.970
you have to be careful there.

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:36.805
And despite the
name, I quibble that.

00:33:36.805 --> 00:33:38.680
It's not really about
language understanding.

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:40.320
It's more about
testing knowledge

00:33:40.320 --> 00:33:43.030
because I think I'm pretty
competent in language

00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:45.030
understanding, and I don't
think I would do that

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:47.910
well at MMLU because I just
don't know random facts

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:51.300
about foreign policy.

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:54.012
And the way they
evaluated at the time

00:33:54.012 --> 00:33:55.470
the state of our
language model was

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.000
GPT-3 using few shot prompting.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.730
So here's what the
prompt looks like.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:02.860
You have a simple instruction.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:07.590
You're given examples--
samples of what the format is.

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:09.370
Compute this.

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:10.630
Here's the answer.

00:34:10.630 --> 00:34:14.250
And then the last
one is the question

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:15.550
with the answer choices.

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:18.750
And the goal is to produce the--

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:20.420
whatever the letter is.

00:34:25.548 --> 00:34:28.570
This was before
instruction tuning.

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:32.440
So you had to really be careful.

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:35.030
You couldn't just say like,
answer this question zero shot.

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:36.780
If you gave it a
question zero shot, based

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:39.389
models would just ask,
generate more questions

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:42.600
or do something weird.

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:51.659
So at that time, the GPT-3
model was getting 45% accuracy.

00:34:51.659 --> 00:34:54.300
Now I'm going to show you this.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:58.990
Let's dive in a little bit
and look at these predictions.

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:02.340
So HELM is a framework
for evaluation

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:07.290
that we built that hosts a
bunch of different evaluations.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:10.010
And the nice thing about
HELM is it allows you

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.495
to-- you look at
the leaderboards.

00:35:11.495 --> 00:35:15.800
You can see how well
models are doing.

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:18.660
So it seems like Claude is
doing pretty well on MMLU.

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:21.620
And you click in, and
you can actually--

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:23.190
let me see the full leaderboard.

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:29.090
So you can see all the
different subjects and MMLU.

00:35:29.090 --> 00:35:31.650
OK.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:34.480
Let's pick one that we all
know something about, computer

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:34.980
science.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:36.350
Good-- OK.

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:38.630
And if you click
through, you can actually

00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:41.520
see all the instances.

00:35:41.520 --> 00:35:43.700
So you have the
input, and then you

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:46.370
have the different
answer choices and then

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:48.170
what the language
model predicted

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:51.860
and then whether it
was correct or not.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:56.370
So here's an example
of an MMLU question.

00:35:56.370 --> 00:36:00.290
And apparently, I
guess Claude did not

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:03.530
get this one right and so on.

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:07.410
One other thing-- I think
if you dive in here,

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:11.390
this actually gives
you the prompt

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:13.350
that was fed into
the language model.

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:15.840
So we're doing
few-shot prompting.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:20.322
So here you have the question,
answer, question, answer,

00:36:20.322 --> 00:36:22.530
question, answer, question,
answer, question, answer.

00:36:22.530 --> 00:36:27.710
This is five-shot and then the
final question where the answer

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:30.150
is meant to be filled in.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:30.650
Yeah.

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:31.820
So I have a question here.

00:36:31.820 --> 00:36:33.750
It seems when you were
doing future prompting,

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:35.930
you have 10 questions
of a similar type

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:37.640
of a similar topic beforehand.

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:40.370
Is there like any
study as to how

00:36:40.370 --> 00:36:42.800
those questions that are
previously in your future

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:45.485
prompt that affect your
performance, the language

00:36:45.485 --> 00:36:47.610
model's performance, the
question you actually ask?

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:49.740
Because it could be that
if it's too similar,

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:52.640
the initial
question, you already

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:53.880
answered the final question.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:55.400
And the second part
of that question

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:57.650
is, do people still
use few-shot prompting

00:36:57.650 --> 00:37:00.170
in evaluating [? ML ?]
new benchmark for learning

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:01.430
a new language?

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:05.150
Yeah, so the first
question is, do the choice

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:07.080
of few-shot examples matter?

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:08.670
And the answer is yes.

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:09.750
They definitely matter.

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.370
The order of them also matters.

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:15.497
The format matters because if
you happen to do classification

00:37:15.497 --> 00:37:17.580
and you choose a bunch of
positive only positives,

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:18.788
then guess what?

00:37:18.788 --> 00:37:21.080
Your language model is just
going to produce positives.

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:26.460
And so five examples need
to be carefully chosen.

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:30.620
And then the second question
is, do people still do few-shot?

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:35.240
Generally, it's-- I mean,
people do zero-shot,

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:38.510
and zero-shot have-- models have
been tuned to make zero-shot

00:37:38.510 --> 00:37:39.360
work.

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:42.050
Few-shot is still
done, sometimes maybe

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:47.300
with one example to
essentially provide the format.

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:51.290
There's some bunch of papers
that analyze whether few-shot

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:52.280
learning is--

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:54.950
in-context learning is
actually learning anything

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:56.870
like because five
examples come out really.

00:37:56.870 --> 00:38:00.320
Are you learning how to do US
history from five examples?

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:04.150
And generally, people agree
that it's more about just

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:11.130
telling you what the format is
and specifying what the task is.

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:13.130
And if you have a good
instruction volume model,

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:14.255
you can just write it down.

00:38:14.255 --> 00:38:16.120
You can say, answer it
with a single letter,

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:17.810
and the model will do that.

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:20.030
So it's becoming rarer.

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:21.880
And also it saves
you token budget

00:38:21.880 --> 00:38:23.890
because you don't
need to have all

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:25.575
these examples in your context.

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:33.400
So that's MMLU.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:36.130
And you notice that
maybe some of you--

00:38:36.130 --> 00:38:38.710
I don't know if anyone who
follows it maybe closely.

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:41.750
Like the highest numbers
are actually in the 90s,

00:38:41.750 --> 00:38:44.870
and this is because
the prompting matters.

00:38:44.870 --> 00:38:47.450
We use a fairly standard
prompt strategy.

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:49.690
But if you're doing
prompting and train

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:52.770
of thought and ensembling, then
you can get higher numbers.

00:38:55.770 --> 00:39:02.490
OK, one, I guess, comment
maybe I'll make right now

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:07.060
is that MMLU was
started in 2020.

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:09.970
Remember, this is really when
there was no instruction model.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:12.820
So it was meant to
evaluate base models.

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:17.550
And right now, it's
used to evaluate-- well,

00:39:17.550 --> 00:39:21.330
whatever the latest models
are, which are primarily

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:22.720
instruction tuned.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:28.650
And I think there's
this worry that,

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:30.760
oh, people are overfit to MMLU.

00:39:30.760 --> 00:39:33.580
And I think that's
certainly true.

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:39.270
But if you look at how MMLU is,
I think, a good evaluation for--

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:41.680
I think of it as a good
evaluation for base models,

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:44.230
because if you think about
what a base model is,

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:47.260
you're just predicting the
next token on some corpus.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:54.580
So if you were able to
magically train on a lot of data

00:39:54.580 --> 00:39:58.120
and be able to do
well on MMLU without--

00:39:58.120 --> 00:39:59.690
basically without even trying--

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:01.720
this is not studying
for the exam

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.470
and doing well on the exam--

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:07.330
then you probably can do--
you probably have good amount

00:40:07.330 --> 00:40:10.390
of quote unquote "intelligence"
and can do a bunch of other

00:40:10.390 --> 00:40:12.110
general things.

00:40:12.110 --> 00:40:17.800
Whereas if you go and you curate
like multiple-choice questions

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:23.620
in the 57 subjects,
then you're probably--

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:25.550
you might get really
good MMU scores,

00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:28.420
but your generality
is probably not

00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:31.400
going to be as much as
you're estimating with MMLU.

00:40:31.400 --> 00:40:34.760
So that's a point on
interpreting this number.

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:36.980
It's really a function
of not just the number,

00:40:36.980 --> 00:40:39.220
but also what if
you're evaluating

00:40:39.220 --> 00:40:43.480
and what the training set is.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:46.490
OK, let's come back to this.

00:40:46.490 --> 00:40:51.370
So over the years,
MMLU has been improved

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:53.750
by a bunch of other benchmarks.

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:58.270
So MMLU-Pro was this paper
that came out last year.

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:01.250
And they basically took MMLU.

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:04.150
They removed some noisy,
trivial questions.

00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:07.940
They said, whoa, everyone's
getting 90% on MMLU.

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:10.030
We can't get everyone
in A, so we're

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:14.090
going to make it 10 choices
instead of four choices.

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:18.820
And the accuracy drops.

00:41:18.820 --> 00:41:23.093
The models drop in accuracy.

00:41:23.093 --> 00:41:24.760
I guess, by this
point, chain of thought

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:28.410
had been fairly common
as a way to evaluate,

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:30.160
which makes a lot of
sense, because if you

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:32.030
look at some of
the MMLU questions,

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:35.240
it's hard to just immediately
output the answer.

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:37.370
You have to think
about it for a bit.

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:40.330
And this is what chain
of thought gives you.

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:43.960
And so the whole point
was that, well, look,

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:47.570
MMLU-Pro scores are lower.

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:52.170
And I guess chain of
thought seem to help,

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:55.730
although not terribly
consistently.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:42:00.450
So MMLU-Pro is, I think, you'll
see a lot of model providers--

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:05.090
developers kind of adopting
MMLU-Pro because you're giving--

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:09.480
you're not in this
saturation region that ML--

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:12.830
at least for
Frontier models-- is.

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:13.538
We can skip that.

00:42:13.538 --> 00:42:15.830
You can click here, and you
can look at the predictions

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:18.530
of MMLU-Pro if you want.

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:21.560
Let's go on to GPQA.

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:27.030
So this is raising
the stakes here.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:31.160
So this is actually maybe
a year or almost one,

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:33.620
1 and 1/2 years ago.

00:42:33.620 --> 00:42:38.210
And here the emphasis
was explicitly

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:43.590
on really hard kind of
PhD-level questions,

00:42:43.590 --> 00:42:46.820
whereas MMLU was just questions
from the internet that

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:50.580
could have been undergrad or
different levels, who knows.

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:52.770
But this was they
recruited explicitly.

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:54.770
People who were getting
their PhDs or had

00:42:54.770 --> 00:42:58.140
finished their PhDs
in a particular area.

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:01.850
And then they had a
fairly elaborate process

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:04.920
for there was someone
who wrote the question,

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:08.490
then you get some expert to
validate it and give feedback,

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:12.080
and then the expert would--

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.810
basically, the question writer
would revise the question

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:17.940
to make it clear, and then the
expert would validate it again.

00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:20.660
And then you give
it to a non-expert

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:28.340
who would spend like around 30
minutes, even without Google,

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:29.880
to try to answer the question.

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:32.360
And it turned out
that experts were

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:37.770
able to get 65% more or
less, and non-experts,

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:41.490
even with Google, can
only get like 30%.

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:44.360
So this is what-- their
attempt to make it

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:51.870
kind of really difficult. That's
why they call it Google proof.

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:53.740
If you search for 30
minutes on Google,

00:43:53.740 --> 00:43:56.970
you're not going
to find the answer.

00:43:56.970 --> 00:44:04.660
OK, so GPT-4, at the
time, got 39% accuracy.

00:44:04.660 --> 00:44:06.270
Now let's look.

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:08.650
So now, this is updated.

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:10.540
So now, O3 is at 75.

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:15.060
So in the last year, there's
been quite a bit of progress

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:16.260
here.

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:20.700
I think the fact that
it's PhD or Google proof

00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:23.070
doesn't mean that
language models can't

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:27.210
do a good job on this.

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:30.330
So one thing--
let me just like--

00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:31.565
I don't know-- click in.

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:37.740
So they have this
thing where you're not

00:44:37.740 --> 00:44:40.510
meant to put this on the web.

00:44:40.510 --> 00:44:44.640
So we have this
little decrypt thing

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:48.990
that allows you have to
type in to manually view it.

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:53.220
So here's an example
of a question.

00:44:53.220 --> 00:44:55.440
I'm definitely not
expert at this,

00:44:55.440 --> 00:45:00.030
so I don't know but seems
like a question to me.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:05.770
And you'll see
that actually for--

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:07.240
so this is O3.

00:45:07.240 --> 00:45:09.000
Actually, the only
thing about O3

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:11.440
is that it basically hides
all the chain of thought.

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:14.350
So we don't get to look at that.

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:24.510
If you look at
Gemini, then I think

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.100
you can see the prediction.

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:32.470
So this is the question,
some biology question.

00:45:32.470 --> 00:45:38.532
And Gemini will break down the
rationale and think for a while.

00:45:38.532 --> 00:45:40.240
And then it says the
correct answer is D.

00:45:40.240 --> 00:45:41.655
And it happens to be right.

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:46.900
Yeah.

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:50.800
Well, when you answered, because
the focus on this-- the focus

00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:53.803
is Google proof,
how do you know?

00:45:53.803 --> 00:45:54.470
How do you know?

00:45:54.470 --> 00:45:56.053
Let's say when it's
a black box model,

00:45:56.053 --> 00:45:58.960
like [INAUDIBLE]
or OpenAI models,

00:45:58.960 --> 00:46:01.970
that they are not themselves
searching the web per se,

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:03.565
trying to find the answer?

00:46:03.565 --> 00:46:05.440
And when you're evaluating
words with respect

00:46:05.440 --> 00:46:07.038
to any human
benchmark, how do we

00:46:07.038 --> 00:46:08.830
know that the human is
not using a language

00:46:08.830 --> 00:46:09.872
model in the first place?

00:46:09.872 --> 00:46:13.630
Like a Google benchmark may
not be a [INAUDIBLE] benchmark.

00:46:13.630 --> 00:46:18.430
Yeah, so the question is,
is it really foolproof?

00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:21.610
Meaning that if you
call O3, maybe O3

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:24.310
is secretly calling
the internet.

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:26.560
I mean, certainly, you have
to be careful because some

00:46:26.560 --> 00:46:33.057
of the endpoints, they
do search the web,

00:46:33.057 --> 00:46:35.390
but there's also a mode where
they don't search the web.

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:40.060
So I think we just may use the
one that doesn't search the web.

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:43.520
And I mean, you have to trust
that that's what's happening.

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:49.500
And then regarding getting
human-level accuracy,

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:52.460
you're saying maybe the
non-experts actually use Google

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:58.850
and used like O3 or something.

00:46:58.850 --> 00:47:00.390
It's possible.

00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:02.090
I don't know exactly how they--

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:03.890
I mean, I think you
just tell them not to,

00:47:03.890 --> 00:47:05.010
and you're paying them.

00:47:08.098 --> 00:47:08.640
I don't know.

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:10.430
You can monitor them.

00:47:10.430 --> 00:47:13.400
I guess it's a little bit tricky
because now Google Gemini,

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:16.310
even if you're using Google,
it shows you answers.

00:47:16.310 --> 00:47:19.850
So yeah, it's a good point.

00:47:19.850 --> 00:47:22.160
And do you have a question?

00:47:22.160 --> 00:47:25.580
Yeah, I was just going
to say experts also

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:30.120
do still achieve [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:31.380
So it's surprising.

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:38.180
But a lot of times, I do
wonder about [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:38.180 --> 00:47:40.335
Yeah.

00:47:40.335 --> 00:47:41.960
It seems like, to
me, that we're slowly

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:45.103
targeting more and more
expert-driven question.

00:47:45.103 --> 00:47:47.270
It seems like we're trying
to make the models better

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:50.270
for a smaller and smaller
subset of the population.

00:47:50.270 --> 00:47:52.850
Is there any research shows
that as these models get

00:47:52.850 --> 00:47:57.300
at these more and more
like expert-level problems

00:47:57.300 --> 00:47:59.593
that they actually also go
to the general populace?

00:47:59.593 --> 00:48:01.760
Yeah, so the question is,
it seems like all of these

00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:04.310
are very elite questions.

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:07.970
And what about the rest of
the people in the world?

00:48:07.970 --> 00:48:10.310
We're going to see a
little bit later that--

00:48:10.310 --> 00:48:13.110
I mean, this is only
one slice of a lecture.

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:14.790
There's going to
be other things.

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:19.760
I mean, I guess
one perspective--

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:24.920
I think the reason why people
focus on these type of questions

00:48:24.920 --> 00:48:27.990
is that experts are expensive.

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:31.710
And so if you can
solve these tasks,

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:33.590
then the idea is that
if you're general, then

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:38.300
you can actually do
fairly complicated work.

00:48:38.300 --> 00:48:39.330
But you're right.

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:41.590
I mean, there's other
things that, let's say,

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:46.590
responding to simple questions
or doing customer service

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:50.550
support, which don't
require a PhD, that

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:52.150
are still nonetheless valuable.

00:48:52.150 --> 00:48:56.490
And I'll come back to
talking about how we might

00:48:56.490 --> 00:48:58.440
address some of those issues.

00:48:58.440 --> 00:49:01.150
OK, let me move on in
the interest of time.

00:49:01.150 --> 00:49:04.630
So final kind of
crazy hard problem--

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:08.700
it's called
Humanity's Last Exam.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:10.360
What a great name.

00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:15.600
So again, there's a
lot of questions here.

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:18.480
This one's multimodal now.

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:21.790
But it's still multiple
choice, short answer.

00:49:21.790 --> 00:49:23.760
So these are still
exam-like questions

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:28.000
that have a correct
answer, which is, I think,

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:30.887
an important limitation
because there are often

00:49:30.887 --> 00:49:32.970
things that we ask about
which are vague and don't

00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:34.150
have a right answer.

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:38.250
So this is definitely
just one subset.

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:41.260
And they did
something interesting.

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:43.020
They created a prize
pool to encourage

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:47.340
people to create problems,
and they offered co-authorship

00:49:47.340 --> 00:49:48.790
to question creators.

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:51.070
So they got quite
a few questions,

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:54.990
which they used to use the
frontier language models

00:49:54.990 --> 00:50:00.177
to reject the questions that
were, quote unquote, "too easy."

00:50:00.177 --> 00:50:01.510
And they did a bunch of reviews.

00:50:01.510 --> 00:50:04.390
So each of these is
like fairly time--

00:50:04.390 --> 00:50:09.190
really, really time-consuming
to create these data sets.

00:50:09.190 --> 00:50:13.570
And every one of these data
set graphs looks like this.

00:50:13.570 --> 00:50:15.840
Previous benchmarks, they--

00:50:15.840 --> 00:50:16.840
LLMs do well.

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:19.650
My new benchmark LLMs do poorly.

00:50:19.650 --> 00:50:23.910
And right now, I
think HLE is up to--

00:50:23.910 --> 00:50:28.360
I want to say like 20%.

00:50:28.360 --> 00:50:29.773
So let's look at the latest.

00:50:32.430 --> 00:50:34.510
Yeah, so O3 is getting 20.

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:37.990
So I assume this
will only just go up

00:50:37.990 --> 00:50:40.900
with in the next--
next year, but I

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:42.680
know this is supposed
to be the last exam,

00:50:42.680 --> 00:50:45.460
so I don't know what's
going to come after that.

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:47.070
OK, yeah.

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:54.400
[INAUDIBLE] I don't
know about being

00:50:54.400 --> 00:50:56.150
able to propose a
reasonable alternative.

00:50:56.150 --> 00:50:59.540
It's hard to-- sometimes
unfair criticism,

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:03.370
but the way that's designed
is almost the exact inverse

00:51:03.370 --> 00:51:07.390
of how I would design if
I were like [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:07.390 --> 00:51:12.440
just because if you send out
an open call for questions,

00:51:12.440 --> 00:51:17.020
you're going to receive like
a very biased set of people

00:51:17.020 --> 00:51:19.210
responding.

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:22.360
You're going to get
people who are super

00:51:22.360 --> 00:51:25.990
exposed to LLMs already, who
know what questions are supposed

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:28.390
to be easy or supposed
to be difficult,

00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:31.433
are very embedded in
the research already.

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:39.160
You're going to end up with the
most specific set of questions

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:40.960
imaginable.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:44.420
It's hard to think
through, I guess.

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:45.040
Yeah.

00:51:45.040 --> 00:51:48.490
Yeah, so we're basically
saying there's a huge bias here

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:51.730
when you're curating
or soliciting questions

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.350
because who's going to do this?

00:51:54.350 --> 00:51:58.270
Maybe people already know LLMs,
or they have a certain thing.

00:51:58.270 --> 00:51:59.660
Yeah, you're absolutely right.

00:51:59.660 --> 00:52:01.387
There is definitely bias.

00:52:01.387 --> 00:52:03.970
I think the only thing you can
say about these is that they're

00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:07.570
hard, but they're clearly
not representative

00:52:07.570 --> 00:52:11.560
of any particular
distribution of questions

00:52:11.560 --> 00:52:13.790
that people are trying to ask.

00:52:13.790 --> 00:52:16.810
Yeah, OK.

00:52:16.810 --> 00:52:18.695
Quick question.

00:52:18.695 --> 00:52:19.570
Actually, [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:19.570 --> 00:52:21.170
OK, all right.

00:52:21.170 --> 00:52:24.160
So let's talk a little bit
about instruction-following

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:24.830
benchmarks.

00:52:24.830 --> 00:52:26.860
So far, all of
these have basically

00:52:26.860 --> 00:52:32.560
been roughly multiple choice
or short answer questions.

00:52:32.560 --> 00:52:34.060
Obviously, with
multiple choice, you

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.900
can make them as
arbitrarily hard,

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:40.370
and they're very structured.

00:52:40.370 --> 00:52:44.690
So one shift that has happened
over the last four years

00:52:44.690 --> 00:52:48.230
is the emphasis on
instruction following, which

00:52:48.230 --> 00:52:49.890
is popularized by ChatGPT.

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:52.890
You just ask the model to
do stuff, and it does stuff.

00:52:52.890 --> 00:52:56.460
So there's no notion of a
necessarily even like a task.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:53:00.960
You just describe these new
things, new one-off tasks,

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:02.880
and the language
model has to do it.

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:06.380
So one of the main
challenges here is that,

00:53:06.380 --> 00:53:09.810
how do you evaluate an
open-ended response in general?

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:12.120
And this is an unsolved problem.

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:15.000
And I'll show you a few
things that people do.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:17.580
And each of these
has its own problems.

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:21.710
So Chatbot Arena, I
mentioned it before.

00:53:21.710 --> 00:53:27.080
This is probably one of the
most popular benchmarks.

00:53:27.080 --> 00:53:29.180
So the way it works
is that random person

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:30.690
from the internet
types in a prompt.

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:33.210
They get a response
from two models.

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:37.020
They don't know which the
models are coming from,

00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:39.180
and they rate which
response is better.

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:41.640
And then based on these
pairwise rankings,

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.420
ELO scores are
computed, and you get

00:53:44.420 --> 00:53:47.090
a ranking of all of the models.

00:53:47.090 --> 00:53:51.410
So this is the current snapshot
that I just took today.

00:53:51.410 --> 00:53:54.350
What's I think nice about
this is that these are not

00:53:54.350 --> 00:53:55.320
static benchmarks.

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:57.150
It's a static prompt.

00:53:57.150 --> 00:54:00.840
They were live kind of
coming in and dynamic.

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:07.640
So we are able to always
have fresh data, so to speak.

00:54:07.640 --> 00:54:09.620
And also the ELO
rating allows you

00:54:09.620 --> 00:54:15.750
to accommodate new models
that are coming in,

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:20.670
which is a feature that people
playing-- like chess players,

00:54:20.670 --> 00:54:24.380
I guess, or figure it out.

00:54:24.380 --> 00:54:29.030
So that's Chatbot Arena.

00:54:29.030 --> 00:54:35.190
I don't know how many of you
saw the recent kind of scandal

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:36.870
around Chatbot Arena.

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:42.540
So over the last, I guess, two
or so years, this Chatbot Arena

00:54:42.540 --> 00:54:46.560
has really risen in
prominence to the point where

00:54:46.560 --> 00:54:52.290
Sundar Pichai is like tweeting
about how great Gemini is

00:54:52.290 --> 00:54:54.430
doing on Chatbot Arena.

00:54:54.430 --> 00:54:59.490
So it becomes a target
that mall developers are--

00:54:59.490 --> 00:55:05.370
I mean, whatever they're
doing, they're using it for PR.

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:08.580
And if you know
Goodhart's law, once you

00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:12.030
are able to measure
something, it gets hacked.

00:55:12.030 --> 00:55:15.210
And there was this paper called
"The Leaderboard Illusion"

00:55:15.210 --> 00:55:19.782
that talks about how there's
some providers that actually got

00:55:19.782 --> 00:55:21.990
privileged access, or they
were able to make multiple

00:55:21.990 --> 00:55:22.600
submissions.

00:55:22.600 --> 00:55:27.460
There's a lot of maybe
less than ideal, I guess,

00:55:27.460 --> 00:55:31.580
protocol for evaluation, which
hopefully will be addressed.

00:55:31.580 --> 00:55:35.930
But so there's certainly
problems with the protocol.

00:55:35.930 --> 00:55:39.040
There's also the
question of random people

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:41.350
from the internet
doing this what

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:45.590
distribution does that serve?

00:55:45.590 --> 00:55:46.090
Yeah.

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:51.040
Random in the sense that
it could be anything?

00:55:51.040 --> 00:55:53.240
I don't mean this
in a formal sense--

00:55:53.240 --> 00:55:58.960
random as in whoever happens
to be going to the site.

00:55:58.960 --> 00:56:01.660
Yeah.

00:56:01.660 --> 00:56:04.900
So here is another
evaluation that I

00:56:04.900 --> 00:56:08.980
think is popular called IFEval.

00:56:08.980 --> 00:56:14.380
So the idea here is that this
is going to narrowly test

00:56:14.380 --> 00:56:18.280
the ability of a language
model to follow constraints,

00:56:18.280 --> 00:56:18.830
essentially.

00:56:18.830 --> 00:56:20.622
So they come up with
a bunch of constraints

00:56:20.622 --> 00:56:23.290
like you have to answer
with at least or at most

00:56:23.290 --> 00:56:25.490
some number of
sentences or words.

00:56:25.490 --> 00:56:28.460
And you have to use these words
and not these other words.

00:56:28.460 --> 00:56:30.620
You have to format
it in a certain way.

00:56:30.620 --> 00:56:35.170
And they basically add
these synthetic constraints

00:56:35.170 --> 00:56:36.445
to a bunch of examples.

00:56:38.892 --> 00:56:41.350
The nice thing is that the
constraints can be automatically

00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:43.655
verified with just
like a simple script,

00:56:43.655 --> 00:56:45.280
because you can just
see how many words

00:56:45.280 --> 00:56:46.880
or how many sentences there are.

00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:48.883
So a lot of the IF
evaluations, you

00:56:48.883 --> 00:56:50.800
have to be very careful
because all it's doing

00:56:50.800 --> 00:56:54.260
is evaluating whether it's
following the constraint or not.

00:56:54.260 --> 00:56:57.110
It's not actually evaluating
the semantics of the story.

00:56:57.110 --> 00:57:03.800
So if you generate a story
about a dog and 10 words,

00:57:03.800 --> 00:57:06.530
it will just evaluate, did you
output a story with 10 words,

00:57:06.530 --> 00:57:09.310
not whether the story
was good or not.

00:57:09.310 --> 00:57:13.030
I would think about it
as a partial evaluation,

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:14.850
and certainly it can be gamed.

00:57:17.650 --> 00:57:20.032
And if you look at--

00:57:20.032 --> 00:57:21.740
maybe I don't have
time to go through it,

00:57:21.740 --> 00:57:27.140
but the instructions are,
I would say, maybe not

00:57:27.140 --> 00:57:31.610
the most realistic.

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:34.290
I would just maybe look.

00:57:34.290 --> 00:57:37.290
So I'm planning a trip to
Japan, writing an itinerary.

00:57:37.290 --> 00:57:39.540
You're not allowed to use
commas in your response.

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:41.780
OK, sure.

00:57:41.780 --> 00:57:46.370
Or you have to use at least
12 placeholder tokens.

00:57:46.370 --> 00:57:49.610
So I'm showing you
examples because I

00:57:49.610 --> 00:57:53.000
think it's important
to realize what's

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:55.700
behind these benchmarks when
you see the numbers because most

00:57:55.700 --> 00:57:59.490
of the people just look at
the numbers, and that's it.

00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:05.420
So AlpacaEval is
another benchmark

00:58:05.420 --> 00:58:09.890
where to address the
issue of, how do you

00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:12.060
evaluate open-ended responses?

00:58:12.060 --> 00:58:14.870
Basically, this is
computing a win rate

00:58:14.870 --> 00:58:20.300
against a particular model as
judged by a language model.

00:58:20.300 --> 00:58:23.070
So immediately, I know
someone's going to say, well,

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:23.700
this is bias.

00:58:23.700 --> 00:58:25.490
And yes, it's biased
because you're

00:58:25.490 --> 00:58:29.390
asking GPT-4, how much do
you like this model response

00:58:29.390 --> 00:58:32.060
against your own generation?

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:36.890
But nonetheless, it
seems to be helpful.

00:58:36.890 --> 00:58:38.510
One of the things that--

00:58:38.510 --> 00:58:42.330
just as kind of an
interesting anecdote.

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:47.850
So this came out in 2023,
and then it became popular.

00:58:47.850 --> 00:58:51.950
So a lot of people submitted
these actually smaller models

00:58:51.950 --> 00:58:53.430
that did really well.

00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:55.970
And it turned out that
it was gaming the system

00:58:55.970 --> 00:58:59.540
by just having longer or longer
responses, which fooled GPT-4

00:58:59.540 --> 00:59:01.400
into liking it.

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:03.530
And then so that got
corrected with this kind

00:59:03.530 --> 00:59:06.110
of length corrected variant.

00:59:06.110 --> 00:59:07.430
And the only thing you--

00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:11.030
I think you can really say
here is that this is correlated

00:59:11.030 --> 00:59:17.510
with Chatbot Arena, which means
that well, they're giving you

00:59:17.510 --> 00:59:18.480
the same information.

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:19.290
This is automatic.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:20.610
The other one involves humans.

00:59:20.610 --> 00:59:22.830
So kind of I guess pick your--

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:28.450
if you wanted something quick
and automatic and reproducible,

00:59:28.450 --> 00:59:32.520
AlpacaEval is a
reasonable choice.

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:37.480
There's kind of another
benchmark called WildBench,

00:59:37.480 --> 00:59:40.800
which the utterances
come from a bunch

00:59:40.800 --> 00:59:43.920
of human-bot conversations.

00:59:43.920 --> 00:59:46.270
They put out a bot
basically for people to use,

00:59:46.270 --> 00:59:48.760
and they collected the data
and made a data set out of it.

00:59:48.760 --> 00:59:53.040
Again, this is using LLM as a
judge, now with a checklist,

00:59:53.040 --> 00:59:56.760
so that it basically has
to think about the response

00:59:56.760 --> 01:00:00.090
and make sure that it
covers certain aspects.

01:00:00.090 --> 01:00:03.660
And this is also correlated
with a Chatbot Arena.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:07.710
So interesting that
evaluation of evaluation

01:00:07.710 --> 01:00:11.250
is correlation with
Chatbot Arena in this--

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:13.680
in this space.

01:00:13.680 --> 01:00:17.940
So moving on.

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:19.900
Let's talk about agents a bit.

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:24.005
So some tasks require
tool use-- for example,

01:00:24.005 --> 01:00:24.880
you have to run code.

01:00:24.880 --> 01:00:28.410
You have to access
the internet, or you

01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:30.570
have to use a calculator--

01:00:30.570 --> 01:00:34.120
and involve iterating
over some period of time.

01:00:34.120 --> 01:00:37.183
So if you're writing and
working on a project,

01:00:37.183 --> 01:00:38.350
it's not an immediate thing.

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:41.170
You have to do it for a while.

01:00:41.170 --> 01:00:43.480
And so this is where
agents come in.

01:00:43.480 --> 01:00:45.150
So agents are
basically-- there's

01:00:45.150 --> 01:00:48.750
a language model and some
of agent scaffolding, which

01:00:48.750 --> 01:00:51.720
is basically some programmatic
logic for deciding how

01:00:51.720 --> 01:00:54.210
the language model gets called.

01:00:54.210 --> 01:00:57.000
And I'm going to talk
about three different agent

01:00:57.000 --> 01:01:00.610
benchmarks, just to give you a
flavor of what that looks like.

01:01:00.610 --> 01:01:04.770
There's SWEBench where you're
given a codebase and a GitHub

01:01:04.770 --> 01:01:06.130
issue description.

01:01:06.130 --> 01:01:09.240
You're supposed to
submit a PR, and the goal

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:14.420
is to submit the PR change
that makes the unit tests pass.

01:01:14.420 --> 01:01:15.670
So it kind of looks like this.

01:01:15.670 --> 01:01:18.520
Here's the issue.

01:01:18.520 --> 01:01:20.900
And it would give the
language model the code,

01:01:20.900 --> 01:01:23.950
and the language model
generates a patch.

01:01:23.950 --> 01:01:25.990
And then you run the tests.

01:01:25.990 --> 01:01:31.640
So this has been very popular
for evaluating agent benchmarks.

01:01:31.640 --> 01:01:34.870
Here's another one
called CyBench.

01:01:34.870 --> 01:01:39.410
And this is for
doing cybersecurity.

01:01:39.410 --> 01:01:41.860
So the idea is that there's
these capture-the-flag

01:01:41.860 --> 01:01:46.760
competitions where an agent
has access to a server,

01:01:46.760 --> 01:01:48.640
and the goal is to
basically have the agent

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:51.790
hack into the server and
retrieve some secret key.

01:01:51.790 --> 01:01:55.690
And if it can do that,
it solves the challenge.

01:01:55.690 --> 01:02:00.700
To do that, the agent
essentially has to run commands.

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:05.120
Here's the agent
architecture, which is fairly,

01:02:05.120 --> 01:02:10.930
I think, standard in this
space where it basically

01:02:10.930 --> 01:02:14.800
asks the language model to
think about it and make a plan

01:02:14.800 --> 01:02:16.580
and generate a command.

01:02:16.580 --> 01:02:20.000
The command gets executed, and
that updates the agent's memory.

01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:23.590
And then it iterates and does
it again and then iterates

01:02:23.590 --> 01:02:26.950
until you either run out of
time or you've successfully

01:02:26.950 --> 01:02:29.680
completed the task.

01:02:29.680 --> 01:02:38.080
On these agent benchmarks, the
accuracies are still fairly low.

01:02:38.080 --> 01:02:40.700
Now it's, I guess, up to 20%.

01:02:40.700 --> 01:02:44.810
But the thing is that not all
the tests are created equal.

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:47.960
There is a first
solve time by humans.

01:02:47.960 --> 01:02:50.960
So how long did it take a
team of humans to solve it?

01:02:50.960 --> 01:02:54.740
The longest challenge
took 24 hours.

01:02:54.740 --> 01:02:58.450
So now O3 is able
to solve something

01:02:58.450 --> 01:03:00.880
that took humans 42 minutes.

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:04.930
So it'll be interesting to
monitor what happens here.

01:03:04.930 --> 01:03:10.310
MLEBench is another agent
benchmark, which is interesting.

01:03:10.310 --> 01:03:13.268
It's 75 Kaggle
competitions where

01:03:13.268 --> 01:03:15.810
you're given a description of
the Kaggle competition and data

01:03:15.810 --> 01:03:20.280
set, and the agent is meant
to write code, train a model,

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:22.990
debug, change the
hyperparameters,

01:03:22.990 --> 01:03:25.957
and then submit.

01:03:25.957 --> 01:03:27.790
I mean, for those of
you who've done Kaggle,

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:30.360
it's basically an
agent that does Kaggle.

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:38.260
And again, the accuracies are
in sub-20, I think, for getting,

01:03:38.260 --> 01:03:40.170
let's say, any metal,
which is some threshold

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:42.030
for-- of performance.

01:03:42.030 --> 01:03:46.200
Even the best models are
getting pretty low accuracy

01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:46.960
at this point.

01:03:46.960 --> 01:03:50.380
So it'll be interesting to
see what happens, I guess,

01:03:50.380 --> 01:03:53.370
in the next year.

01:03:53.370 --> 01:03:56.370
One thing I-- benchmark
I did want to mention

01:03:56.370 --> 01:04:00.550
is this out in left field a bit.

01:04:00.550 --> 01:04:03.360
All the tasks that
we've discovered

01:04:03.360 --> 01:04:06.250
have some anchoring in
you need more knowledge.

01:04:06.250 --> 01:04:08.130
You need linguistic knowledge.

01:04:08.130 --> 01:04:11.550
And the question is, can
you isolate the knowledge

01:04:11.550 --> 01:04:16.600
and factor that out and focus
exclusively on this reasoning?

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:19.710
And you can argue that
reasoning captures a more

01:04:19.710 --> 01:04:21.310
pure form of intelligence.

01:04:21.310 --> 01:04:22.900
It's not just memorizing facts.

01:04:22.900 --> 01:04:26.550
So we want to reward models
for creativity and ability

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:29.760
to solve new things rather
than just I saw the internet,

01:04:29.760 --> 01:04:32.740
and therefore I'm able
to do these tasks.

01:04:32.740 --> 01:04:37.060
So there's something called
the ARC AGI challenge.

01:04:37.060 --> 01:04:39.060
It was actually introduced by--

01:04:39.060 --> 01:04:46.380
in 2019, pre-LLMs,
which is interesting.

01:04:46.380 --> 01:04:49.420
So here's one of the tasks.

01:04:49.420 --> 01:04:51.240
So you're given
basically these patterns,

01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:55.500
and you're basically
trying to fill in this one.

01:04:55.500 --> 01:04:59.700
So these are meant
to be easy for humans

01:04:59.700 --> 01:05:02.550
to detect what the pattern is.

01:05:02.550 --> 01:05:06.660
But there's no
there's no language,

01:05:06.660 --> 01:05:08.170
and there's no task description.

01:05:08.170 --> 01:05:14.100
And so the language
model actually is--

01:05:14.100 --> 01:05:16.750
traditionally has been
pretty really bad on these.

01:05:16.750 --> 01:05:18.760
So this is the accuracy.

01:05:18.760 --> 01:05:24.520
And you see that
GT-40 is basically 0.

01:05:24.520 --> 01:05:30.060
And now we're seeing kind of
O3 basically doing pretty well

01:05:30.060 --> 01:05:31.740
on this task.

01:05:31.740 --> 01:05:35.680
Now, per task, it's
costing quite a bit.

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:37.200
It's using a lot of compute--

01:05:37.200 --> 01:05:40.660
over probably like a few
hundred dollars to do that.

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:44.988
But I guess if you put
that much compute into it,

01:05:44.988 --> 01:05:46.780
you're able to do well
on this leaderboard.

01:05:46.780 --> 01:05:50.620
And now there's an AGI-2, which
is even a harder benchmark.

01:05:50.620 --> 01:05:54.130
So this is one class
of benchmarks that's,

01:05:54.130 --> 01:05:58.110
I guess, in a bit
in left field, but I

01:05:58.110 --> 01:06:03.540
think it captures something
pure about intelligence

01:06:03.540 --> 01:06:06.480
that others aren't doing.

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:11.110
Bringing it back to
I guess, reality,

01:06:11.110 --> 01:06:13.820
let me talk a little bit
about safety benchmarks.

01:06:13.820 --> 01:06:16.750
So the idea of safety
benchmarks is-- well,

01:06:16.750 --> 01:06:18.160
we do them in other fields.

01:06:18.160 --> 01:06:23.510
Like we do crash testing
and safety ratings for cars,

01:06:23.510 --> 01:06:25.790
and we do safety
for food and so on.

01:06:25.790 --> 01:06:29.810
So what would it
mean to do it for AI?

01:06:29.810 --> 01:06:32.390
So there's again
no clear answer.

01:06:32.390 --> 01:06:35.230
AI is so early that I
think people haven't really

01:06:35.230 --> 01:06:38.440
figured out what safety means,
but nonetheless, there's

01:06:38.440 --> 01:06:41.530
a bunch of work here
maybe to mention.

01:06:41.530 --> 01:06:43.990
There's a paper
called HarmBench,

01:06:43.990 --> 01:06:49.880
which essentially they figure
out 510 harmful behaviors,

01:06:49.880 --> 01:06:57.520
and they essentially prompt a
language model to see if it can

01:06:57.520 --> 01:06:59.540
will follow the instructions.

01:06:59.540 --> 01:07:01.840
So, for example, given
detailed instructions

01:07:01.840 --> 01:07:06.850
for making dimethylmercury
from [INAUDIBLE] access

01:07:06.850 --> 01:07:10.480
to a special tool, and
the correct answer is yes.

01:07:10.480 --> 01:07:13.000
Sorry, I can't do that.

01:07:13.000 --> 01:07:15.270
But there are
other cases where--

01:07:18.940 --> 01:07:21.980
oops, maybe there's
no 0 on here.

01:07:21.980 --> 01:07:27.920
OK, well, if you
look at the ratings,

01:07:27.920 --> 01:07:30.590
I guess they're doing
reasonably well.

01:07:30.590 --> 01:07:32.140
But some of these
models obviously

01:07:32.140 --> 01:07:34.960
are complying and not--

01:07:34.960 --> 01:07:41.170
like DeepSeek V3 is happily
to give you instructions.

01:07:41.170 --> 01:07:43.570
So there's another
benchmark called AIR-Bench

01:07:43.570 --> 01:07:47.380
where I think makes the idea
of safety a little bit more

01:07:47.380 --> 01:07:48.140
grounded.

01:07:48.140 --> 01:07:51.700
So they looked at different
regulatory frameworks

01:07:51.700 --> 01:07:54.040
and company policies
and built a taxonomy

01:07:54.040 --> 01:07:59.650
of the different types of
things that constitute safety.

01:07:59.650 --> 01:08:01.360
So this is anchoring
safety, which

01:08:01.360 --> 01:08:05.800
is the abstract concept and
actually law and policies

01:08:05.800 --> 01:08:11.360
and then building a
benchmark mark around this.

01:08:11.360 --> 01:08:16.800
And so let me just quickly
take a look at this.

01:08:16.800 --> 01:08:22.170
So you can see that Claude
seems to be pretty reasonable,

01:08:22.170 --> 01:08:26.160
refusing to comply with a bunch
of things, though not perfect.

01:08:26.160 --> 01:08:28.160
And you see that some
of the other models

01:08:28.160 --> 01:08:34.250
are maybe less good at it.

01:08:34.250 --> 01:08:37.760
OK, one important thing I think
to discuss when you're thinking

01:08:37.760 --> 01:08:40.290
about safety is jailbreaking.

01:08:40.290 --> 01:08:44.130
And this is like a
meta safety thing,

01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:48.439
because language models
are trained to refuse

01:08:48.439 --> 01:08:49.620
harmful instructions.

01:08:49.620 --> 01:08:55.200
But you can actually bypass
the safety if you're clever.

01:08:55.200 --> 01:09:01.069
So there's this paper
that developed a procedure

01:09:01.069 --> 01:09:05.279
to essentially optimize the
prompt to bypass safety.

01:09:05.279 --> 01:09:07.890
They did it on actually open
weight model, the Llama model.

01:09:07.890 --> 01:09:10.260
And it actually
transfers the GPT-4.

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:14.060
So you feed in a prompt,
which is a step-by-step plan

01:09:14.060 --> 01:09:16.700
to destroy humanity, and
then some gibberish, which

01:09:16.700 --> 01:09:18.540
is automatically optimized.

01:09:18.540 --> 01:09:24.510
And then ChatGPT will
happily give you a plan.

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:27.950
So no, of course, I don't
think you can actually follow

01:09:27.950 --> 01:09:29.279
this and destroy humanity.

01:09:29.279 --> 01:09:32.840
So you could argue
that maybe this is not

01:09:32.840 --> 01:09:36.830
the most realistic example, but
nonetheless, the fact that you

01:09:36.830 --> 01:09:38.810
could bypass a
safety intervention

01:09:38.810 --> 01:09:41.970
means that if there
were more, I guess,

01:09:41.970 --> 01:09:46.050
serious high stakes issues,
then this might be a problem.

01:09:46.050 --> 01:09:47.180
Yeah, question.

01:09:47.180 --> 01:09:50.600
Question about the safety
net or [INAUDIBLE] refusal

01:09:50.600 --> 01:09:53.960
rate that you were showing.

01:09:53.960 --> 01:09:58.430
I was wondering if this is a
comprehensive-- if this also

01:09:58.430 --> 01:10:00.320
takes into account,
for example, let's say,

01:10:00.320 --> 01:10:03.110
the language model just like
refuses to answer anything.

01:10:03.110 --> 01:10:04.840
Like that wouldn't
be very helpful.

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:05.340
Yeah.

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:06.030
Yeah.

01:10:06.030 --> 01:10:06.708
Yeah.

01:10:06.708 --> 01:10:08.250
So the question is
like-- yes, you're

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:11.820
absolutely right that it's easy
to be the top of the leaderboard

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:14.680
by just saying I don't know, or
I can't do that for everything.

01:10:14.680 --> 01:10:18.450
So typically, you
have to pair this

01:10:18.450 --> 01:10:21.910
with a capabilities eval that
shows that the language model--

01:10:21.910 --> 01:10:25.870
yes, indeed, it actually does
something, and also, it's safe.

01:10:25.870 --> 01:10:26.370
Yeah.

01:10:29.790 --> 01:10:30.820
OK.

01:10:30.820 --> 01:10:37.920
So a quick note about
pre-deployment testing.

01:10:37.920 --> 01:10:40.980
The Safety Institutes
from the US and UK

01:10:40.980 --> 01:10:44.250
and some other countries
that have established

01:10:44.250 --> 01:10:49.380
this voluntary protocol
with model developers,

01:10:49.380 --> 01:10:53.070
such as Anthropic and OpenAI,
where the company will give them

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:56.430
early access to a
model pre-release

01:10:56.430 --> 01:10:58.930
so that they can run a
bunch of safety evaluations,

01:10:58.930 --> 01:11:00.870
generate a report, and
then essentially give

01:11:00.870 --> 01:11:02.730
feedback to inform
the deployment

01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:04.450
procedure of the company.

01:11:04.450 --> 01:11:05.508
So this is not binding.

01:11:05.508 --> 01:11:06.550
There's no law around it.

01:11:06.550 --> 01:11:08.285
It's just voluntary for now.

01:11:12.990 --> 01:11:17.130
And basically, these evaluations
use some of the same evaluations

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:19.800
like that we've
been talking about.

01:11:19.800 --> 01:11:22.870
But I think there's a
broader question here,

01:11:22.870 --> 01:11:26.020
which is what exactly is safety?

01:11:26.020 --> 01:11:29.397
And you quickly
after you-- we didn't

01:11:29.397 --> 01:11:31.480
get a chance to really
look at all the utterances,

01:11:31.480 --> 01:11:33.630
but you quickly realize
that a lot of safety

01:11:33.630 --> 01:11:35.200
is strongly contextual.

01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:37.990
They depend on the law and
politics and the social norms,

01:11:37.990 --> 01:11:41.760
and they might vary
across country.

01:11:41.760 --> 01:11:45.690
You might think that
safety is about refusal,

01:11:45.690 --> 01:11:48.840
and it is at odds with
capability because the more safe

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:51.160
you are, the more you refuse,
and less helpful you are.

01:11:51.160 --> 01:11:53.760
But that's not quite true
because safety is broader

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:55.890
than just refusal,
hallucinations,

01:11:55.890 --> 01:12:00.690
and some of medical setting
or high stakes setting is bad.

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:02.440
Actually, reducing
hallucinations

01:12:02.440 --> 01:12:06.230
makes systems more capable and
more safe, not hallucinations.

01:12:09.200 --> 01:12:11.200
I mean, another
thing that's relevant

01:12:11.200 --> 01:12:14.390
is there's capabilities,
and there's propensity.

01:12:14.390 --> 01:12:18.190
So capabilities is the
ability for a language model

01:12:18.190 --> 01:12:19.520
to do it at all.

01:12:19.520 --> 01:12:23.230
Propensity is whether
it's been basically--

01:12:23.230 --> 01:12:25.850
can refuse not to
do things right.

01:12:25.850 --> 01:12:28.130
So often, the base model
will have the capabilities

01:12:28.130 --> 01:12:30.490
and the alignment
part, which we'll

01:12:30.490 --> 01:12:33.640
talk about in a week
or two, is the thing

01:12:33.640 --> 01:12:39.220
that makes the language models
have less propensity to do harm.

01:12:39.220 --> 01:12:42.940
So what you care
about, what does--

01:12:42.940 --> 01:12:44.800
it matters.

01:12:44.800 --> 01:12:47.930
Sorry, it depends on the regime.

01:12:47.930 --> 01:12:49.990
So if you just
have an API model,

01:12:49.990 --> 01:12:52.210
that only propensity
matters because you can only

01:12:52.210 --> 01:12:55.750
access the model if it
refuses but actually knows

01:12:55.750 --> 01:13:00.260
how to cause harm,
that's fine as long

01:13:00.260 --> 01:13:02.660
as you can't be jailbroken.

01:13:02.660 --> 01:13:04.700
But for open wave
models, then capability

01:13:04.700 --> 01:13:06.200
matters as well,
because people have

01:13:06.200 --> 01:13:08.540
shown that you can just
turn off the safety fairly

01:13:08.540 --> 01:13:11.540
easily via fine tuning.

01:13:11.540 --> 01:13:14.210
And to make things
more complicated,

01:13:14.210 --> 01:13:18.500
the Safety Institute
was using CyBench

01:13:18.500 --> 01:13:21.980
to do cybersecurity
safety because they

01:13:21.980 --> 01:13:24.690
were worried about cyber risk.

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:27.170
What happens if a malicious
actor was able to use LLMs

01:13:27.170 --> 01:13:30.750
to agents to hack into systems?

01:13:30.750 --> 01:13:33.950
But on the other
hand, agents can

01:13:33.950 --> 01:13:38.090
be really helpful for
doing penetration testing

01:13:38.090 --> 01:13:40.190
before you deploy a system.

01:13:40.190 --> 01:13:44.690
So these kind of dual
use issues make it

01:13:44.690 --> 01:13:47.210
so that it's
actually-- capabilities

01:13:47.210 --> 01:13:50.396
and safety are really
kind of intertwined.

01:13:53.750 --> 01:13:57.720
So let me quickly
just go through this.

01:13:57.720 --> 01:14:00.690
So I think a question
was brought up earlier

01:14:00.690 --> 01:14:01.720
about realism.

01:14:01.720 --> 01:14:05.520
So language models are used
quite a bit in practice.

01:14:05.520 --> 01:14:08.920
But these benchmarks, especially
the standardized exam,

01:14:08.920 --> 01:14:11.920
are pretty far away from
real world use case.

01:14:11.920 --> 01:14:14.580
And you might think, oh,
well, as long as we get

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:16.180
real live traffic, we're good.

01:14:16.180 --> 01:14:19.650
But it turns out that many
times, people are just

01:14:19.650 --> 01:14:21.690
messing with you and doing--

01:14:21.690 --> 01:14:23.800
giving you spammy utterances.

01:14:23.800 --> 01:14:27.900
So that's not exactly the
distribution you want.

01:14:27.900 --> 01:14:31.180
I think there's really
two types of prompts.

01:14:31.180 --> 01:14:36.340
The question is, are you asking
me, or are you quizzing me?

01:14:36.340 --> 01:14:39.613
So quizzing, the user
already knows the answer,

01:14:39.613 --> 01:14:41.280
but it's just trying
to test the system,

01:14:41.280 --> 01:14:43.890
and asking if the user
doesn't know the answer

01:14:43.890 --> 01:14:47.320
is trying to get the system to
use it to use it-- to get it.

01:14:47.320 --> 01:14:51.300
And of course, asking
prompts are more realistic

01:14:51.300 --> 01:14:55.590
and produces value
for the user, which

01:14:55.590 --> 01:14:58.350
means that standardized
exams, I think,

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:05.220
clearly are not realistic but
nonetheless can be helpful.

01:15:05.220 --> 01:15:08.160
So there's this
paper from Anthropic

01:15:08.160 --> 01:15:14.590
that uses language models
to analyze real world data.

01:15:14.590 --> 01:15:16.030
So let me just show you.

01:15:16.030 --> 01:15:19.620
So they take a bunch
of conversations,

01:15:19.620 --> 01:15:23.680
and they use language models to
essentially hardcore cluster.

01:15:23.680 --> 01:15:26.790
And they find basically
a distribution

01:15:26.790 --> 01:15:30.790
over what people are
using the cloud for.

01:15:30.790 --> 01:15:36.420
And coding is one of the
top, as you might imagine.

01:15:36.420 --> 01:15:41.220
So one thing that's
interesting is

01:15:41.220 --> 01:15:45.330
that once you deploy a system,
you actually have the data,

01:15:45.330 --> 01:15:48.390
and you have the means
to actually evaluate

01:15:48.390 --> 01:15:54.150
on realistic use cases, because
these are people paying your--

01:15:54.150 --> 01:15:55.930
to use your API.

01:15:55.930 --> 01:16:02.460
So they must at least care a
little bit about the response.

01:16:02.460 --> 01:16:10.020
So there's also a project
called MedHELM where we have--

01:16:10.020 --> 01:16:13.080
so previous medical
benchmarks were essentially

01:16:13.080 --> 01:16:16.740
based on these
standardized exams.

01:16:16.740 --> 01:16:25.170
Here there were 29
clinicians who were asked,

01:16:25.170 --> 01:16:28.590
what are the real world
use cases in your practice

01:16:28.590 --> 01:16:31.740
where language models
could be useful?

01:16:31.740 --> 01:16:36.990
You got 121 clinical
tasks, and they

01:16:36.990 --> 01:16:41.670
produced a bunch-- a different--
a wide suite of benchmarks

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:48.030
that tested for these
more realistic use cases,

01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:52.650
such as writing up a
patient notes or planning

01:16:52.650 --> 01:16:54.195
treatments and so on.

01:16:56.920 --> 01:17:02.660
So this benchmark, actually,
you can see it on HELM as well.

01:17:02.660 --> 01:17:07.220
But some of the data sets
involve patient data.

01:17:07.220 --> 01:17:10.960
So therefore, obviously,
they're not hosted publicly.

01:17:10.960 --> 01:17:14.330
So that's one kind of tension
that you have to deal with,

01:17:14.330 --> 01:17:18.970
which is that realism
and privacy are at odds.

01:17:18.970 --> 01:17:22.130
So let's talk about
validity here.

01:17:22.130 --> 01:17:26.230
So train-test overlap, that five
minutes of a lecture and someone

01:17:26.230 --> 01:17:27.260
asked about that.

01:17:27.260 --> 01:17:29.600
So you know not to
train on your test set.

01:17:29.600 --> 01:17:32.530
And previously, we didn't have
to think very much about this

01:17:32.530 --> 01:17:35.830
because some benchmark
designer carefully divided

01:17:35.830 --> 01:17:37.600
train and test.

01:17:37.600 --> 01:17:39.258
And nowadays, people
train internets,

01:17:39.258 --> 01:17:41.300
and they don't tell you
about what their data is.

01:17:41.300 --> 01:17:45.460
So this is basically impossible.

01:17:45.460 --> 01:17:48.790
Route one, what you can
do is you can be clever,

01:17:48.790 --> 01:17:52.720
and you try to infer whether
your test set was trained on

01:17:52.720 --> 01:17:55.120
by trying to query the model.

01:17:55.120 --> 01:17:57.310
There's some kind of
interesting tricks

01:17:57.310 --> 01:18:02.800
that you can use by noticing
that if the language

01:18:02.800 --> 01:18:06.880
model prescribes a
certain type of order,

01:18:06.880 --> 01:18:08.740
it favors a certain
type of order

01:18:08.740 --> 01:18:10.930
that correlates with
a data set order then.

01:18:10.930 --> 01:18:14.170
That's a sign that
it's been trained on.

01:18:14.170 --> 01:18:20.030
Route 2 is that you
can encourage norms.

01:18:20.030 --> 01:18:25.090
So there's this paper that
essentially looked at,

01:18:25.090 --> 01:18:31.540
how often was it the case that
when someone, a model provider,

01:18:31.540 --> 01:18:34.810
reported a data
set they actually

01:18:34.810 --> 01:18:38.480
tested, whether their test set
was not in the training set?

01:18:38.480 --> 01:18:44.350
And providers definitely do, but
it's definitely not the norm.

01:18:44.350 --> 01:18:47.920
So you can think about
this as akin to well, you

01:18:47.920 --> 01:18:51.420
report numbers, and you should
report whether confidence

01:18:51.420 --> 01:18:53.200
intervals or standard errors.

01:18:53.200 --> 01:18:57.060
And maybe this is something
that the community

01:18:57.060 --> 01:18:58.510
can work on improving.

01:18:58.510 --> 01:19:01.350
There's also issues
of data set quality.

01:19:01.350 --> 01:19:05.370
SWEBench apparently had
some errors that got fixed.

01:19:05.370 --> 01:19:08.680
Many benchmarks
actually have errors.

01:19:08.680 --> 01:19:11.340
So if you see these scores
like math and [? GSMK, ?]

01:19:11.340 --> 01:19:14.680
they're at like 90-plus percent.

01:19:14.680 --> 01:19:18.460
And you wonder, well, man, those
questions must be really hard.

01:19:18.460 --> 01:19:20.160
And it turns out
that half of them

01:19:20.160 --> 01:19:22.900
are actually just
noise, label noise.

01:19:22.900 --> 01:19:26.350
So once they get fixed,
then the numbers go up.

01:19:26.350 --> 01:19:28.180
OK, final comment.

01:19:28.180 --> 01:19:31.650
So what are we even evaluating?

01:19:31.650 --> 01:19:37.270
Before, we were
evaluating methods.

01:19:37.270 --> 01:19:39.400
Because you fix, train, test.

01:19:39.400 --> 01:19:40.660
You have a new architecture.

01:19:40.660 --> 01:19:42.070
You do a new learning algorithm.

01:19:42.070 --> 01:19:44.040
You train, and then you test.

01:19:44.040 --> 01:19:45.750
And you get some
number that tells you

01:19:45.750 --> 01:19:47.530
how good your method is.

01:19:47.530 --> 01:19:51.410
Today, I think it's an important
distinction that we're not

01:19:51.410 --> 01:19:52.380
evaluating methods.

01:19:52.380 --> 01:19:56.400
We're evaluating systems
where anything goes.

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:58.500
And there's some exceptions.

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:02.300
So not all GPT is a
speed run competition

01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:06.260
where given a fixed data set and
you basically minimize the time

01:20:06.260 --> 01:20:09.240
to get to a particular
loss, and datacomp,

01:20:09.240 --> 01:20:10.760
which you're trying
to select data

01:20:10.760 --> 01:20:13.580
to get you a level of accuracy.

01:20:13.580 --> 01:20:16.340
And these are
helpful for getting--

01:20:16.340 --> 01:20:19.230
encouraging algorithmic
innovation from researchers.

01:20:19.230 --> 01:20:24.150
But evaluating systems is
also really useful for users.

01:20:24.150 --> 01:20:28.310
So again, I think it's important
to define the rules of the game

01:20:28.310 --> 01:20:33.780
and also to think about what is
the purpose of your evaluation.

01:20:33.780 --> 01:20:36.500
So hopefully, that
was a whirlwind tour

01:20:36.500 --> 01:20:38.647
of different aspects
of evaluation.

01:20:38.647 --> 01:20:39.730
Hope that was interesting.

01:20:39.730 --> 01:20:41.520
OK, that's all.

01:20:41.520 --> 01:20:43.420
See you next time.
